actors: Attacker | Victim | Server

step: The attacker gets a session id of their own
msg: Attacker -> Server: GET / (no login)
text: The attacker visits the site without logging in and is handed a
  session id, `FIXED123`. Nothing about it is valuable yet, because nobody is
  logged in behind it.

step: The attacker plants that id in the victim's browser
msg: Attacker -> Victim: link that sets FIXED123
text: A crafted link, a cookie set from a sibling subdomain, or a hostile
  network can all do this. The attacker never touches the victim's password.

step: The victim logs in carrying the planted cookie
msg: Victim -> Server: POST /login + FIXED123
text: The victim types the right password. The server accepts it, and now has
  to decide which session id the login should be attached to.

step: A vulnerable server keeps the id it was given
note: Server: FIXED123 is now the victim's
text: This is the bug in the lab 00 starter. The server reuses the id the
  request came with, so the session the attacker already knows about turns
  into a logged-in one.

step: The attacker uses the id they already had
msg: Attacker -> Server: GET /me + FIXED123
text: The server sees a valid, authenticated session and answers as the
  victim. The only thing that would have stopped this is a login that never
  reuses an id supplied from outside.
